## 1. Introduction: Why C Still Matters

The :contentReference[oaicite:0]{index=0} is one of the most important pillars in modern computing. Despite being created in the early 1970s, it is still actively used in production systems that require maximum performance, deterministic memory behavior, and direct hardware interaction.

C is not simply “an old language.” It is a systems-level abstraction that sits extremely close to machine architecture while still providing a structured programming model. It is the bridge between human-readable logic and processor-executable instructions.

To understand modern software engineering deeply—operating systems, compilers, embedded systems, networking—you must understand C.

