## 2. Historical Evolution of C

### 2.1 Predecessors: BCPL and B

Before C existed, systems programming at Bell Labs relied on:

- BCPL (Basic Combined Programming Language)
- B language (a simplified version of BCPL)

These languages were typeless or weakly typed and heavily constrained by the hardware of the time.

However, they lacked:
- Efficient data structuring
- Strong enough memory control
- Scalability for large operating systems

This created the need for a more powerful systems language.

---

### 2.2 Birth at Bell Labs

C was developed in the early 1970s by Dennis Ritchie at Bell Labs.

Its creation was tightly linked to the development of the UNIX operating system.

Key motivations:
- Rewrite UNIX in a higher-level language instead of assembly
- Maintain performance comparable to assembly
- Enable portability across different hardware systems

This was a radical idea at the time: operating systems were typically written entirely in assembly language, making them tied to specific machines.

C changed this paradigm.

---

### 2.3 UNIX and the Revolution of Portability

When UNIX was rewritten in C:

- The same operating system code could run on multiple machines
- Hardware independence became achievable
- Software engineering became more scalable

This was one of the earliest and most important moments in computing history where abstraction layers became practical at the system level.

C became the language of operating systems.

---

### 2.4 Standardization Era

As C spread beyond UNIX:

- It was widely adopted in academia and industry
- Different compilers introduced inconsistencies
- Standardization became necessary

This led to:
- ANSI C standard (1989)
- ISO C standardization (later revisions: C99, C11, C17, C23)

Each standard introduced improvements:
- Better type safety
- New libraries
- Improved memory handling features
- More predictable behavior across platforms

